# Insertion sort

Grow a sorted prefix one element at a time. Take the first unsorted element,
shift every larger element in the prefix one position right, and drop it into
the gap — the way most people sort a hand of cards.

**Invariant.** Before step $i$, the first $i$ elements are sorted among
themselves, though not necessarily in final position.

## Flow

```d2
direction: down

start: Start {shape: oval}
pick: "key = a[i]; j = i-1"
test: "j >= 0 and a[j] > key?" {shape: diamond}
shift: "a[j+1] = a[j]; j--"
place: "a[j+1] = key"
next: "i++" {shape: hexagon}
done: Sorted {shape: oval}

start -> next
next -> pick: "i < n"
pick -> test
test -> shift: yes
shift -> test
test -> place: no
place -> next
next -> done: "i = n"
```

The inner loop shifts rather than swaps. That is the whole difference from
[[bubble-sort]], and it halves the writes.

## Cost

- **Best** $O(n)$ — already sorted, so the inner test fails immediately every
  time and the algorithm is a single scan.
- **Average and worst** $O(n^2)$.
- **Space** $O(1)$. In-place and stable.

The exact comparison count is the number of **inversions** in the input plus
$n-1$. For nearly sorted data that number is small, and this is the property
that makes insertion sort genuinely useful:

$$
T(n) = \Theta(n + I) \quad \text{where } I = |\{(i,j) : i < j,\; a_i > a_j\}|
$$

!!! tip "Where it actually ships"

    Every serious sort implementation switches to insertion sort on small
    partitions — typically 16 to 32 elements. Below that size its low constant
    factor beats [[merge-sort]] and [[quicksort]] outright, and the recursion
    those two need costs more than the scan it saves. Timsort and introsort both
    do this.

??? note "Binary insertion sort"

    The position for `key` can be found by binary search in $O(\log n)$
    comparisons instead of $O(n)$. It reduces *comparisons* to $O(n\log n)$ but
    leaves *shifts* at $O(n^2)$, so it only helps when comparing is much more
    expensive than moving — comparing long strings, for instance. See
    [[complexity#Counting what matters]].

## Why it is stable

`a[j] > key` is strict, so shifting stops at the first element equal to the key
and the key lands *after* its equals.
